{% callout type="summary" %}
**Quick answer:** The heart is supplied by the right and left coronary arteries, the first branches of the aorta, arising above the aortic valve. The left divides into the LAD and circumflex arteries; in most people the right gives the posterior interventricular artery. Most venous blood drains through the coronary sinus into the right atrium.
{% /callout %}

{% cbmeTags codes="AN22.3" phase="Phase 1" /%}

## Overview: where the heart's blood comes from

Cardiac muscle (myocardium) uses a great deal of energy and relies almost entirely on oxygen-dependent metabolism. It therefore needs a constant, rich blood supply. Only the inner one-tenth of a millimetre of the endocardial surface can take nutrition directly from the blood inside the chambers, so this source is minuscule. Almost all the supply comes through the **coronary arteries**.

Two coronary arteries, right and left, arise from the **ascending aorta** immediately above the aortic valve. They are the first branches of the aorta. Together they form an oblique inverted crown in the **atrioventricular (coronary) groove**, which is why they are called "coronary".

The main arteries and their big branches lie on the surface of the heart, in the subepicardial connective tissue. These surface vessels are the **epicardial arteries**. Smaller branches then penetrate the muscle (**intramural arteries**) and break up into arterioles and a dense capillary network. Each myocyte touches roughly three capillaries.

## Origin, course and branches of the coronary arteries (AN22.3)

The coronary arteries start from **ostia** (openings) in the **aortic sinuses**, the three dilatations of the aortic root just above the aortic valve.

{% figure src="/learn-images/anatomy/blood-supply-of-heart/coronary-arteries-anterior.webp" alt="Front of the heart showing the coronary arteries and main branches." caption="The coronary arteries and their main branches on the front of the heart." credit="BruceBlaus, CC BY 3.0, via Wikimedia Commons" license="CC BY 3.0" source="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blausen_0256_CoronaryArteries_02.png" width="960" height="720" /%}

- The **right coronary artery (RCA)** arises from the **anterior** (right coronary) aortic sinus.
- The **left coronary artery (LCA)** arises from the **left posterior** (left coronary) aortic sinus.

The left artery is the larger of the two in calibre and supplies more myocardium. The diameter of the coronary arteries is about 1.5 to 5.2 mm. Both run in the coronary sulcus.

### Right coronary artery

1. It first passes forward and to the right, emerging between the root of the pulmonary trunk and the right auricle (the ear-like part of the right atrium).
2. It runs down in the right anterior part of the coronary sulcus to the junction of the right and inferior borders of the heart.
3. It winds round the inferior (acute) border onto the diaphragmatic surface. There it runs backward and to the left in the right posterior coronary sulcus to reach the posterior interventricular groove.
4. It ends near the **crux** of the heart, often by anastomosing with the circumflex branch of the left coronary artery. The crux is the point on the back of the heart where the atrioventricular groove meets the interatrial and interventricular grooves.

Branches to know:

- **Right conal (conus) artery:** usually the first branch, supplying the conus arteriosus. In about one-third of hearts it arises on its own from the anterior aortic sinus.
- **Atrial branches** (anterior, lateral and posterior), which mainly supply the right atrium.
- **Sinuatrial (SA) nodal artery:** one of the anterior atrial branches in most cases. It arises from the RCA in about 60% of people and from the left coronary artery in about 40%.
- **Right (acute) marginal artery:** runs along the inferior border.
- **Posterior interventricular (posterior descending) artery:** runs in the posterior interventricular groove. It gives septal perforating branches to the posterior part of the interventricular septum.
- **Atrioventricular (AV) nodal artery:** arises near the crux and supplies the AV node in most people.

### Left coronary artery

The left main stem is short, averaging about 2 to 3 cm. It passes forward between the pulmonary trunk and the left auricle, enters the atrioventricular groove and divides into two branches:

- **Anterior interventricular artery**, also called the **left anterior descending (LAD)** artery. It runs down the anterior interventricular groove towards the apex. In most people it then turns round the apex into the posterior interventricular groove and anastomoses with the posterior interventricular branch of the RCA. In about one-third of people it ends at the apex. It gives **diagonal** branches to the anterolateral wall of the left ventricle and **septal** branches to the septum. A small left conus artery supplies the pulmonary conus.
- **Circumflex artery**, which winds round the left margin of the heart in the coronary sulcus. Its **obtuse marginal** branches supply the lateral and posterior walls of the left ventricle, and it also supplies the left atrium.

Sometimes a third branch, the **ramus intermedius**, arises directly from the left main stem, so the stem trifurcates. This is a normal variant.

## Territories supplied

{% table %}
- **Artery**
- **Main territory**
- **Notes**
---
- **Right coronary artery**
- Right atrium, most of the right ventricle, the inferior (diaphragmatic) surface of the left ventricle, the posterior third of the interventricular septum
- Also supplies the SA node in about 60% of people and the AV node in most people
---
- **Left anterior descending**
- Anterior wall of the left ventricle, most of the apex, the anterior two-thirds of the interventricular septum, and an adjoining strip of the right ventricle
- Considered the most important vessel for the surgeon
---
- **Circumflex**
- Left atrium and the lateral (and often posterior) wall of the left ventricle
- Supplies the SA node in some people
---
- **Posterior interventricular artery**
- Posterior part of the septum and often part of the inferior wall of the left ventricle
- Its origin decides dominance
{% /table %}

Put together, the left coronary artery supplies most of the left ventricle, the anterior two-thirds of the septum and most of the left atrium. The right coronary artery covers most of the right side and the diaphragmatic part of the left ventricle.

## Coronary dominance

**Dominance** (the word means which artery gives the posterior interventricular, or posterior descending, artery) describes the pattern of supply to the back of the heart. It has nothing to do with which artery is larger.

- **Right dominant:** the RCA gives the posterior descending artery. This is found in about 85% of people.
- **Left dominant:** the posterior descending artery arises from the circumflex artery, in about 5% of people.
- **Codominant:** the remaining people, about 10%, have the posterior descending vessel supplied from both sides.

In a left dominant heart the circumflex artery wraps round the atrioventricular groove and continues down as the posterior descending artery. It then also supplies the AV node in many cases.

{% callout type="tip" %}
**Dominance check:** ask "which artery gives the posterior interventricular artery?" That artery is dominant. In most people it is the RCA.
{% /callout %}

## Anastomoses and "functional end arteries"

The right and left coronary arteries have many small connections (**anastomoses**) at the arteriolar level, and anastomoses are abundant in the fetus. They are much reduced by the end of the first year of life. Even so, the coronary arteries behave as **functional end arteries**: one artery cannot make up for the other when a branch is suddenly blocked. A sudden block of a large branch usually causes death of the muscle it supplies (myocardial infarction). Sometimes collateral circulation (blood reaching the area by these small connections) is enough to keep the muscle alive. Collaterals can become prominent when disease narrows an artery slowly.

## Venous drainage

Most blood from the heart wall drains into the right atrium through the **coronary sinus**. This is a large, dilated vein lying in the posterior part of the atrioventricular groove. It opens into the right atrium just to the left of the inferior vena cava. About three-quarters of the total coronary blood flow returns this way.

Three main veins open into the coronary sinus. Each typically runs with a coronary artery.

{% table %}
- **Vein**
- **Accompanies**
- **Notes**
---
- **Great cardiac vein**
- Anterior interventricular artery
- Begins at the apex, ascends in the anterior interventricular groove, then follows the atrioventricular groove to the left and backward to enter the coronary sinus. Receives the left marginal vein.
---
- **Middle cardiac vein**
- Posterior interventricular artery
- Runs in the posterior interventricular groove
---
- **Small cardiac vein**
- Right marginal artery
- Runs along the inferior border
{% /table %}

Not all venous blood goes through the coronary sinus:

- **Anterior cardiac veins** drain the anterior part of the right ventricle and open directly into the right atrium. Most venous blood from the right ventricle returns this way.
- The **right marginal vein** runs along the inferior border and usually opens separately into the right atrium.
- **Thebesian veins** are tiny veins that open directly into all the cardiac chambers.
- The **oblique vein of the left atrium** is a small vein that runs on the back of the left atrium and joins the coronary sinus.

{% callout type="tip" %}
**Vein and artery pairs:** great cardiac vein goes with the anterior interventricular artery, middle cardiac vein with the posterior interventricular artery, small cardiac vein with the right marginal artery.
{% /callout %}

## Coronary blood flow

- At rest, coronary blood flow is about 225 mL per minute, which is about 4% to 5% of the total cardiac output.
- Most flow to the myocardium occurs in **diastole** (the relaxation phase of the ventricle), after the aortic valve closes, when the small vessels in the muscle are not squeezed by contraction. At rest diastole takes about two-thirds of the cardiac cycle.
- In a fast heart rate (tachycardia) diastole becomes relatively shorter, so heart perfusion can be compromised.
- In the left ventricle, the vessels inside the muscle are compressed in systole. Flow to the subendocardial layer tends to fall then, but the extra vessels of the subendocardial plexus normally compensate. This difference matters in some types of coronary ischaemia (reduced blood supply to the muscle).
- The right ventricle contracts far less forcefully, so its flow changes less during the cardiac cycle.

## Nerve supply of the heart and the coronary arteries

The heart has an extrinsic supply (autonomic nerves) and an intrinsic supply (its own conducting system). The cardiac cycle starts in the SA node itself, and autonomic nerves adjust its rate and force.

- **Sympathetic nerves** come from the upper four or five thoracic spinal segments (T1 to T5). Stimulation speeds the heart, increases the force of contraction and **dilates the coronary arteries**.
- **Parasympathetic nerves** come from the vagus. They slow the heart.
- Both sets of fibres form the superficial and deep **cardiac plexuses**, and branches run along the coronary arteries to reach the myocardium.

**Cardiac pain** travels along sympathetic fibres, mostly through the left sympathetic trunk to spinal segments T1 to T5. The pain is therefore referred to the skin supplied by those segments: behind the sternum and the medial side of the upper limbs.

## Development in brief

The coronary arteries were once thought to sprout from the aortic root. It is now thought that the vessels form first from mesenchymal cells in the subepicardial space and spread over the heart. Sprouts of this network then reach the base of the outflow tract and connect to the aortic sinuses. In the end, one right and one left coronary artery remain. In up to one-third of hearts, small accessory coronary branches arise directly from the aortic sinuses. The coronary veins develop by sprouting from the coronary sinus.

## Applied anatomy

**Myocardial infarction.** The commonest cause of a blocked coronary artery is an acute thrombosis (clot) on top of a chronic atherosclerotic narrowing. Because the arteries are functional end arteries, a sudden block of a large branch usually causes necrosis (death) of the muscle it supplies. Atherosclerosis can also reduce the blood supply to the conducting system and cause defective conduction.

**ECG and the territory involved.** An inferior wall infarct produces new Q waves in leads II, III and aVF. An anterior wall Q wave infarct interrupts the normal growth of the R wave across the chest leads. The inferior (diaphragmatic) wall of the left ventricle is usually supplied by the RCA, and the LAD supplies the anterior wall and septum.

**Coronary artery bypass grafting (CABG).** Narrowing of more than 60% is likely to reduce perfusion significantly. Patients with severe disease, for example of the left main stem or of all three major vessels with poor left ventricular function, are usually considered for bypass. The grafts commonly used are the internal thoracic (mammary) and radial arteries, and the saphenous vein. The left internal thoracic artery and radial artery grafts stay open better than vein grafts: roughly 40% to 50% of vein grafts have blocked by 10 years, against about 10% of the arterial grafts.

**Anomalies.** There are many variants of coronary anatomy, and most cause no problems.

- Absence of the left main stem is the most common congenital absence. The LAD and circumflex then arise from separate ostia in the left sinus. It is benign and is an occasional finding at angiography.
- Origin of a coronary artery from the pulmonary artery is clinically important. It leads to poorly oxygenated or reversed coronary flow and can cause ischaemia, infarction or sudden death in infancy. Infants typically present at about 6 weeks, when pulmonary artery pressure has fallen, with distress on feeding that is presumed to be angina.
- Coronary anomalies are rare in the general population, yet they are the second most common cause of sudden cardiac death among young athletes.

## Practice MCQs

{% mcq answer="C" explanation="The right coronary artery arises from the anterior (right coronary) aortic sinus, and the left from the left posterior aortic sinus. Both sinuses lie just above the aortic valve." %}
The right coronary artery arises from the:
- A. Left posterior aortic sinus
- B. Pulmonary trunk
- C. Anterior aortic sinus
- D. Arch of the aorta
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="B" explanation="Most myocardial flow occurs in diastole, when the aortic valve is closed and the small vessels are not compressed by contraction. A fast heart rate shortens diastole, which can compromise perfusion." %}
Why can a fast heart rate compromise coronary perfusion?
- A. The coronary arteries constrict in systole only
- B. Diastole, when most coronary flow occurs, becomes relatively shorter
- C. The aortic valve stays open for longer
- D. Venous drainage through the coronary sinus stops
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="D" explanation="Dominance is defined by the artery that gives the posterior interventricular (posterior descending) artery. In most people this is the right coronary artery." %}
Coronary dominance is decided by which artery gives rise to the:
- A. Sinuatrial nodal artery
- B. Left anterior descending artery
- C. Right marginal artery
- D. Posterior interventricular artery
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="A" explanation="The middle cardiac vein runs in the posterior interventricular groove with the posterior interventricular artery. The great cardiac vein goes with the anterior interventricular artery, and the small cardiac vein with the right marginal artery." %}
The middle cardiac vein accompanies which artery?
- A. Posterior interventricular artery
- B. Anterior interventricular artery
- C. Right marginal artery
- D. Circumflex artery
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="C" explanation="The coronary arteries anastomose at arteriolar level, but one cannot make up for the other when a branch is suddenly blocked. A sudden block of a large branch usually causes infarction of the area it supplies." %}
The coronary arteries are called functional end arteries because:
- A. They have no branches
- B. They end in the coronary sinus
- C. Their anastomoses cannot usually compensate for a sudden block of a large branch
- D. They carry blood only during systole
{% /mcq %}

{% mcq answer="B" explanation="The LAD supplies the anterior two-thirds of the interventricular septum through its septal branches. The posterior third is supplied by the posterior interventricular artery." %}
The anterior two-thirds of the interventricular septum is supplied by:
- A. Right marginal artery
- B. Left anterior descending artery
- C. Posterior interventricular artery
- D. Left conus artery
{% /mcq %}

## Viva questions

{% viva question="Where do the coronary arteries arise?" %}
From the ascending aorta, in the aortic sinuses just above the aortic valve. The right arises from the anterior aortic sinus and the left from the left posterior aortic sinus. They are the first branches of the aorta.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Describe the course of the right coronary artery." %}
It passes forward between the pulmonary trunk and right auricle, runs down the right anterior coronary sulcus to the inferior border, winds round to the diaphragmatic surface, and runs in the right posterior coronary sulcus to the posterior interventricular groove. It ends at the crux, often by anastomosing with the circumflex artery.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Name the branches of the left coronary artery." %}
The short left main stem divides into the anterior interventricular (LAD) artery and the circumflex artery. The LAD gives diagonal and septal branches. The circumflex gives obtuse marginal branches. Occasionally a ramus intermedius arises between them.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="What is coronary dominance?" %}
It is decided by the artery that gives the posterior interventricular (posterior descending) artery. About 85% of people are right dominant, about 5% left dominant and about 10% codominant.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Which arteries supply the SA node and the AV node?" %}
The SA nodal artery arises from the RCA in about 60% of people and from the left coronary artery in about 40%. The AV node is supplied by the AV nodal branch of the RCA in most people.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="How does venous blood leave the heart?" %}
Mostly through the coronary sinus into the right atrium, via the great, middle and small cardiac veins. Anterior cardiac veins open directly into the right atrium, and thebesian veins open directly into the chambers.
{% /viva %}

{% viva question="Why does a blocked coronary artery cause infarction?" %}
The coronary arteries are functional end arteries. Their anastomoses are usually too small to make up for a sudden block of a large branch, so the muscle it supplies dies.
{% /viva %}

